from reportlab.lib.pagesizes import letter
from reportlab.platypus import SimpleDocTemplate, Paragraph, Spacer, Image, Table, TableStyle
from reportlab.lib.styles import ParagraphStyle
from reportlab.lib import colors
from reportlab.lib.units import inch
out='output/Dsouza_Coval_Voice_Benchmarks_Scoped_Analysis.pdf'
doc=SimpleDocTemplate(out,pagesize=letter,leftMargin=.5*inch,rightMargin=.5*inch,topMargin=.35*inch,bottomMargin=.3*inch,
    title='Scoped Benchmark Analysis: Coval TTS/STT Voice-AI Benchmarks',author='Craig ML Dsouza')
h=ParagraphStyle('h',fontName='Helvetica-Bold',fontSize=13.5,leading=15.5)
by=ParagraphStyle('by',fontName='Helvetica',fontSize=8,leading=10,textColor=colors.HexColor('#333333'),spaceAfter=1)
sub=ParagraphStyle('s',fontName='Helvetica',fontSize=6.8,leading=8.3,textColor=colors.HexColor('#555555'),spaceAfter=2)
b=ParagraphStyle('b',fontName='Helvetica',fontSize=6.9,leading=8.35,spaceAfter=2)
sc=ParagraphStyle('sc',parent=b,backColor=colors.HexColor('#f2f4f7'),borderPadding=(3,4,3,4),spaceAfter=4,spaceBefore=2)
st=[Paragraph('Scoped Benchmark Analysis: Coval TTS / STT Voice-AI Benchmarks',h),
 Paragraph('<b>Craig ML Dsouza</b> &nbsp;|&nbsp; November 20, 2026',by),
 Paragraph('Data: <i>coval-benchmarks.json</i>, openbenchmarks-labs/tts-stt-benchmarks-by-coval (snapshot synced 2026-11-19; 24h / 7d / 30d windows, 30d shown). Methodology reference: <i>docs/methodology.md</i> in the same repository.',sub),
 Paragraph('<b>Scope.</b> This note re-analyzes only the aggregate statistics the benchmark publishes (mean, p50, p90, p95, p99, min, max and sample count per model and window). It asks three questions: (1) how much of the published ranking survives uncertainty, (2) how sensitive STT conclusions are to the choice of latency metric, and (3) how much of the WER signal comes from a few extreme items. It does not re-run any provider, evaluate cost, or inspect individual transcripts, and no per-item results were used.',sc),
 Image('output/fig.png',width=7.2*inch,height=7.2*inch*6.0/7.6),
 Paragraph('<b>Method.</b> (D) Linear mixed model with <i>model</i> as a random intercept and <i>window</i> as a fixed effect, fit by REML on log p50 latency and on mean WER, using models present in all three windows. (A, B) Parametric-free bootstrap: each model is resampled from its own published quantile curve (piecewise-linear through min, p50, p90, p95, p99, max; WER rescaled to the published mean), 2,000 replicates. Latency uses the reported sample count; WER uses an effective n of 30 (TTS prompts) and 50 (STT utterances, per the methodology document) to allow for prompt-level clustering. A model is on the frontier in a replicate if no other model is both faster and lower-WER; label values are the share of replicates in which it is. Models with fewer than 300 samples and the disabled <i>atlas-tts</i> row (74.7% WER, n = 291) are excluded from frontiers.',b),
 Paragraph('<b>1. Latency separates the models; WER mostly does not (TTS).</b> Between-model variance is 98% of TTS TTFA variance (ICC 0.98; between-model SD 0.78 log units vs 0.12 window-to-window), so speed rankings are stable. WER is not separable: 32 of 33 TTS models have a median WER of exactly 0, the between-model SD is 0.32 points, and 95% CIs are roughly 1.2-1.5 points wide on each side. For example, nari qwen3-tts-fast is faster than ElevenLabs eleven_v4_turbo with probability 1.00 (64 vs 190 ms) but has lower WER with probability 0.43 (difference +0.15 points, 95% CI -1.95 to +2.11). Five TTS models sit on the point-estimate frontier; only fluxions/vui is on it in every replicate (1.00), and the other four have 0.59 or less.',b),
 Paragraph('<b>2. STT conclusions depend on which latency metric is used.</b> The file lists TTFS as the STT latency metric, while the methodology document defines TTFT; the two agree at Spearman rho = 0.63 (n = 27). soniox stt-rt-v6 moves from 6th to 20th, nari qwen3-asr-fast from 4th to 17th and reson8 realtime-global from 3rd to 12th when ranked by TTFT instead of TTFS; baseten whisper-large-v3 moves from 16th to 4th. Nari qwen3-asr-fast is faster than AssemblyAI universal-3.6-pro on TTFS (48 vs 105 ms) but slower on TTFT (1,752 vs 1,118 ms). TTFT also varies only 2% between windows (TTFS: 13%), which may indicate it is bounded by clip duration rather than model speed; this is a hypothesis the aggregates cannot test.',b),
 Paragraph('<b>3. WER is driven by tail events.</b> Reconstructed distributions place about 60% of mean WER in the worst 10% of items (median across models), with roughly half of items scoring exactly 0. 22 of 28 STT models record a maximum WER of 100% or more, consistent with insertion or repeated-output errors, and baseten qwen3-tts-1.7b records 489% on a single item. Mean-WER comparisons are therefore sensitive to one to three items. The only pairwise WER gap among the comparisons checked whose 95% CI excludes zero is Deepgram nova-3 vs AssemblyAI universal-3.6-pro: +2.9 points (CI +0.3 to +5.7), with nova-3 faster on TTFT.',b),
 Paragraph('<b>Limitations.</b> Distributions are reconstructed from at most six published quantiles, and items are treated as exchangeable within the stated effective n, so CIs are approximate and likely somewhat optimistic. A per-task random-effects model needs per-item rows, which the public file does not contain. The repository has no price data, so cost-based comparisons are out of scope. Attributions in (2) and (3) are inferred from distribution shape, not from inspected outputs.',b),
 Paragraph('<b>Items worth confirming with the maintainers.</b> (a) The latest run records dataset_id <i>stt-wildasr-clipping</i> with status PARTIAL, whereas the methodology document describes <i>stt-v2</i> (WildASR fleurs_clean); confirming which set the published STT WER uses would help interpretation. (b) A definition of TTFS alongside TTFT. (c) Publishing per-item results, which would allow tail items to be named and per-task effects estimated.',b)]
doc.build(st)
